  "response_text": "Modifying the OS to always choose the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If one or more high-priority threads never yield or are frequently runnable, the scheduler will never select lower-priority threads, effectively preventing them from ever executing. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of multitasking, where all threads are supposed to get CPU time. Round-robin scheduling avoids this problem by giving each thread a fair chance to run. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging or time slicing, a purely priority-based scheduler can make the system unfair and unpredictable.\\n",
